304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Studies suggest that women presenting with pain may, on average, wait longer for pain relief in some emergency settings and are more likely to have their symptoms attributed to stress or psychological causes. This doesn't happen everywhere, or to everyone, but the pattern is documented often enough to matter.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (What makes this bias so stubborn is that it's rarely deliberate. It lives in language, in stereotypes, in medical education, and in decades-old assumptions about who feels what. It can shape the judgment of any clinician, including women physicians, precisely because no one chose it.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Recognizing that is not an accusation. It's the first step in correcting it and one of the most fascinating places where psychology and medicine meet.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
